-Archives are pruned by hand today.
+`bin/archive-logs.sh` packs the logs of each named service from `logs/` into a dated
+tarball (`SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz`) under `archives/`. It runs from cron on the log host
+every night, from the repository root (`logs/` and `archives/` are relative paths).
+
+- Several services can be given in one run: `bin/archive-logs.sh api web`.
+  Service names are validated first (letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`); an invalid name
+  or a service with no log directory stops the run with a non-zero status.
+- `--keep-days N` (N a positive integer; `--keep-days=N` also works) removes archives
+  older than N days, but only after all new archives are in place. Only
+  `SERVICE-YYYYMMDD.tar.gz` files of the services named in that run are considered, so
+  other services' archives and unrelated files are left alone. Age is the file's mtime.
+- On success it prints a one-line summary, for example:
+  `archived 2 service(s): api web; pruned 3 archive(s) older than 30 days`
+  (the `pruned` part appears only with `--keep-days`). Errors go to stderr.
+
+Exit status: 0 on success, 64 for usage errors, 1 for other failures.
+
+Archives are created with mode 0640 (umask 027).
